From a3f79329fc193afe34ce69feb1b6690b07aaf618 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alberto Gallegos <alberto.gallegos.muro@intel.com>
Date: Thu, 9 Jul 2026 18:28:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add XPU support to FBGEMM sparse ops and jagged tensor tests

Add XPU device support to misc_ops_test.py and permute_sparse_features_test.py.
Includes support for permute_1D_sparse_data and permute_2D_sparse_data operators.
Also adds XPU support to cumsum_test.py for asynchronous_complete_cumsum,
asynchronous_exclusive_cumsum, and asynchronous_inclusive_cumsum operators.
Also adds XPU support to block_bucketize_test.py for the
block_bucketize_sparse_features and block_bucketize_sparse_features_inference
operators.

Adds XPU support to jagged/jagged_index_select_2d_test.py for the
jagged_index_select_2d_forward operator; the backward + opcheck sections are
skipped on XPU because only the forward kernel has an XPU implementation.

Add focused correctness tests for get_infos_metadata in
split_embeddings_utils_test.py and generate_vbe_metadata_test.py. Import
fbgemm_xpu to register the XPU implementation and compare accelerator dispatch
results against the CPU implementation through the shared device-agnostic
accelerator helpers.

Enable expand_into_jagged_permute_test.py on the current accelerator using the
shared accelerator_available/current_accelerator helpers and device-agnostic
tensor transfers.

Also adds XPU support to sparse/reorder_batched_test.py for the
reorder_batched_ad_lengths and reorder_batched_ad_indices operators, including
their *_large_grid regression tests. Those two allocate B = 2**27 elements and
were guarded by gpu_memory_lt_gb, which reads torch.cuda unconditionally and so
short-circuits to "memory is sufficient" on any non-CUDA accelerator instead of
checking one. Add accelerator_memory_lt_gb, which follows torch.accelerator, and
gate the two tests on it so they exercise the grid-stride path on XPU. The
remaining accelerator tests in that file stay gated on gpu_unavailable, so they
keep running on CUDA and skip automatically on XPU:
reorder_batched_sequence_embeddings and its *_large_grid counterpart have no XPU
implementation.

Adds XPU support to the jagged tensor tests covered by the jagged ops port:
dense_to_jagged_test.py, 2d_to_dense_test.py, 1d_to_dense_test.py and
elementwise_binary_test.py, plus the shared jagged/common.py which now imports
fbgemm_xpu so every test module in that directory gets the XPU registrations.
The dense_to_jagged, jagged_to_padded_dense, jagged_2d_to_dense and
jagged_1d_to_dense tests run on the accelerator through the device-agnostic
accelerator helpers.

Four operators in these files have no XPU kernel. stacked_jagged_1d_to_dense and
stacked_jagged_2d_to_dense need fbgemm::offsets_range and a cub::DeviceScan
equivalent, so those two tests are skipped. jagged_dense_elementwise_add and
jagged_dense_elementwise_mul are handled differently: the three tests in
elementwise_binary_test.py sample operation from {add, add_jagged_output, mul}, and
only add_jagged_output has an XPU kernel, so the sampled set is narrowed to
add_jagged_output rather than skipping the tests. All three then run and keep
covering the implemented operator, including the vectorized Half fast path and the
torch.compile dynamic-shape path.

Signed-off-by: Alberto Gallegos <alberto.gallegos.muro@intel.com>
Signed-off-by: Felipe Leza Alvarez <felipe.leza.alvarez@intel.com>

Co-authored-by: Felipe Leza Alvarez <felipe.leza.alvarez@intel.com>
